        <h2 class="section-title">Executive Summary</h2>
        <p>This report analyzes 92 days of quality audit data (October 1 - December 31, 2025) covering three critical accuracy categories—<strong>Business, Compliance, and Customer</strong>—for the Dispatch & Track-and-Trace process, alongside daily Customer Satisfaction (CSAT) survey results.</p>
        <p>Business Critical Accuracy is the clear outlier and the primary driver of customer dissatisfaction during the period. It averaged 87.1% against Compliance (98.0%) and Customer (95.8%) critical accuracy, and it fell as low as 58% on several audit days in October. A single failure reason—agents applying the wrong TMS pickup status ("Missed Pickup Status")—accounts for over half (50.6%) of all Business-Critical failures, concentrated disproportionately on the third shift.</p>
        <p><strong>The good news:</strong> the metric is trending in the right direction. Business Critical Accuracy improved from 79.9% in October to 95.4% in December (R² = 0.357, statistically significant, p < 0.001), and Business Critical Accuracy correlates strongly with daily CSAT (r = 0.88, R² = 0.78) meaning further gains here will have an outsized, measurable effect on customer satisfaction. Compliance and Customer accuracy remained consistently high throughout and show no meaningful relationship with CSAT swings, confirming that Business Critical is where corrective effort should concentrate.</p>

