    <div id="tab-interpretation" class="tab-content">
        <h2 class="section-title">3. Data Interpretation</h2>
        
        <h3 class="subsection-title">3.1 What does the audit data show?</h3>
        <p>Business Critical Accuracy is the standout problem metric. It is the lowest of the three categories in every month of the period, the most volatile day-to-day (standard deviation of ~9.9 points vs. ~3-4 points for Compliance and Customer), and the only category with a fail rate above 10% (12.6% overall, peaking near 20% in October).</p>
        <p>Compliance Critical (98.0%) and Customer Critical (95.8%) accuracy are both stable and within a normal operating band across all three months, with only mild, statistically weak upward movement (R² = 0.018 and 0.158 respectively). Neither shows the large day-to-day swings seen in Business Critical.</p>

        <h3 class="subsection-title">3.2 Is there a valid trend?</h3>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Business Critical Accuracy:</strong> Yes—a statistically significant improving trend (slope &approx; +0.22 pts/day, R² = 0.357, p < 0.001). Monthly averages step up cleanly: 79.9% &rarr; 86.0% &rarr; 95.5%. This is the strongest and most decision-relevant trend in the dataset.</li>
            <li><strong>Customer Critical Accuracy:</strong> A weaker but still significant upward trend (R² = 0.158, p < 0.001), rising from 94.0% to 97.9% across the quarter.</li>
            <li><strong>Compliance Critical Accuracy:</strong> No statistically meaningful trend (R² = 0.018, p = 0.20); performance is flat and consistently strong.</li>
            <li><strong>CSAT:</strong> Both the CSAT weighted average (R² = 0.197, p < 0.001) and CSAT Top-2-Box (R² = 0.266, p < 0.001) show significant upward trends, while CSAT Bottom-2-Box declines significantly (R² = 0.154, p < 0.001)—satisfaction is improving in lockstep with Business Critical Accuracy.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3 class="subsection-title">3.3 Outlier Dates Analysis</h3>
        <p>Four audit days register as statistical outliers in Business Critical Accuracy (z-score > 2). Three of the four fall in October, reinforcing that the month was the epicenter of the issue rather than a uniformly bad quarter. Notably, each of these four dates also recorded some of the lowest CSAT scores in the entire dataset—direct, same-day evidence linking Business Critical failures to customer dissatisfaction.</p>
        
